/strong>, fewer than the 74 that chance alone would give, and <strong>97 record highs</strong>, more than the 74 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (88). Waterloo's all-time high is 112\u00b0F, on July 13, 1936.","t_chg":"No clear change in Waterloo's highs or lows","p_chg":"Across 129 complete years: Waterloo's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.15) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Waterloo's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.12 to +0.07) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Waterloo has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Waterloo's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.34 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.66), about +4.4 inches since 1896. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 36.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (54.15 in); the driest, 1910 (17.35 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}